       requirements: constraint-04
       code evidence: addressed
         7.1  src/acceptance/benchmark/fixtures.py#@@ -104,21 +104,48 @@ def materialize_archetype(fixture_dir: Path, dest: Path) -> MaterializedFixture:
       test evidence: unsupported  [tier: static]
         (no mapped test)
         recommended test: Changing only on-disk modification times does not change the commit contents recorded by materialization.
           inputs:  Use a fixture where the only difference between base and head is file content, while metadata is held constant or intentionally preserved: same filename, same file size, same mode, and same mtime after copy. The test must run materialization under a git config that makes stat-based reuse plausible (`core.checkStat=minimal`, `core.trustctime=false`) so a defective implementation that relies on metadata can record the wrong blob.
           detects: Materialization stages with `git add -A` against a stale index/stat cache and therefore reuses the base blob when the replacement file has the same size, mode, and mtime as the original.
           full detail: acceptance recommendation --criterion content-alone-determines-commit-records

  8. What a commit records does not change when git is configured to compare a working-tree file against its recorded status using fewer fields.
       requirements: constraint-05
       code evidence: addressed
         8.1  tests/benchmark/test_fixtures.py#@@ -85,6 +87,104 @@ def test_materialization_is_deterministic(fixture_dir, tmp_path):
       test evidence: unsupported  [tier: static]
         (no mapped test)
         recommended test: With git configured to compare working-tree files using fewer fields, the recorded commit contents remain the same.
           inputs:  Use the same fixture materialization twice: once with default git comparison behavior and once with fewer working-tree comparison fields configured (for example `core.checkStat=minimal` and `core.trustctime=false`). The fixture must include a same-size replacement file so the reduced-field comparison can misclassify it as unchanged.
           detects: The implementation’s correctness depends on git’s stat comparison settings, so when git is configured to compare fewer fields it silently records different content or a different SHA.
           full detail: acceptance recommendation --criterion fewer-working-tree-fields-do-not-change-recorded-content

